>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Never Run Out of Power on the Road</h2>
  <p>
    My laptop died at an airport gate in Lisbon. Three hours before a client call. No outlets anywhere. I ended up
    buying an overpriced power bank from one of those kiosks. It lasted maybe one charge before dying completely.
  </p>
  <p>
    That $45 lesson taught me something: cheap power banks are worthless when you actually need them. And if you're
    working remotely, you <em>will</em> need one eventually.
  </p>
  <p>
    So I spent four months testing 10 different chargers across Portugal, Spain, and Thailand. Airports, cafes, trains,
    beaches. Basically anywhere I found myself working. Some of these things surprised me. Others were garbage.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The short version:</strong> The <a href="#anker-737">Anker 737 PowerCore</a> is what I actually use now.
    140W charges my MacBook at full speed. Genuinely useful. If you just need phone charging, the <a
      href="#anker-magsafe">Anker 622 MagGo</a> snaps onto iPhones magnetically. Pretty slick. Tight budget? The <a
      href="#iniu-budget">INIU</a> at $20 is shockingly decent for the price.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Picks -->
  <h2 id="quick-picks">The Winners (If You're in a Hurry)</h2>
  <p>Don't want to read 3,000 words about batteries? Fair. Here's what you need:</p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={quickPicksColumns}
      rows={quickPicksRows}
      caption="Top 3 portable chargers for travel"
  />
  <p>
    Still here? Good. I've got detailed reviews of all 8 chargers below, plus the stuff about TSA rules that'll save you
    from having your expensive power bank confiscated at security.
  </p>

  <!-- Understanding Capacity -->
  <h2 id="understanding-capacity">What Do These Numbers Actually Mean?</h2>
  <p>
    mAh, Wh, watts... honestly, it's confusing. Here's what actually matters when you're staring at Amazon specs:
  </p>

  <h3>Real-World Translation</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>5,000mAh:</strong> One full phone charge. That's it. Emergency backup territory.</li>
    <li><strong>10,000mAh:</strong> 2-3 phone charges. Daily carry for most people.</li>
    <li><strong>20,000mAh:</strong> 4-5 phone charges OR about 50% of a laptop battery. This is the sweet spot for
      travel.
    </li>
    <li><strong>25,000+mAh:</strong> Full laptop charge plus phones. Heavy but worth it if you're off-grid a lot.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The Dirty Secret About Capacity</h3>
  <p>
    That 20,000mAh on the box? You'll get maybe 13,000-17,000mAh to your devices. Why?
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Energy lost converting voltage</li>
    <li>Heat (you'll notice they get warm)</li>
    <li>Cable inefficiency</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not a scam. Just physics. Budget for 65-85% of advertised capacity actually reaching your devices.
  </p>

  <!-- Detailed Reviews -->
  <h2 id="detailed-reviews">Every Power Bank I Tested (Honest Takes)</h2>

  <!-- Anker 737 PowerCore -->
  <h3 id="anker-737">1. Anker 737 PowerCore . The One I Actually Use</h3>
  <p>
    This thing lives in my backpack now. Has for about six months.
  </p>
  <p>
    140W. That's wall-outlet territory. My MacBook Pro doesn't even know it's running off a battery. Charges just as
    fast. I ran a lot of tests because I'm that kind of person. Video calls with 30 Chrome tabs open? Fine. Uploading
    huge files? Fine. The little display tells you how much power's left and when your device will hit full charge. I
    look at it way more than I need to.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The specs:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Capacity:</strong> 24,000mAh (86.4Wh)</li>
    <li><strong>Output:</strong> 140W USB-C, 18W USB-A × 2</li>
    <li><strong>Input:</strong> 140W USB-C (refills itself in 1.5 hours)</li>
    <li><strong>Ports:</strong> 2× USB-C, 1× USB-A</li>
    <li><strong>Weight:</strong> 1.3 lbs (632g)</li>
    <li><strong>Dimensions:</strong> 6.1" × 2.2" × 1.9"</li>
    <li><strong>TSA:</strong> Approved (under 100Wh)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>140W is no joke. Charges any laptop I've tried</li>
    <li>That display actually tells you useful stuff</li>
    <li>Recharges fast (faster than my laptop honestly)</li>
    <li>Build quality feels premium, not cheap plastic</li>
    <li>TSA-approved, so no weird conversations at security</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>$110 stings. No way around that.</li>
    <li>1.3 lbs adds up in a bag you carry all day</li>
    <li>Way too big for pockets. Backpack only.</li>
    <li>Only one USB-A port (most stuff is USB-C now anyway)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Price:</strong> $110 retail, but I've seen it drop to $90-100 during sales. Worth setting a price alert.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Who should get it:</strong> Anyone who works from random places and can't afford a dead laptop. The $110
    pays for itself the first time you save a deadline.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/anker-737"
      text="Check Anker 737 Price on Amazon"
      variant="primary"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <!-- Anker 622 MagGo -->
  <h3 id="anker-magsafe">2. Anker 622 MagGo . If You're an iPhone Person</h3>
  <p>
    Okay, this one's clever. It just... snaps onto the back of your iPhone. Magnets. No cables, no fumbling, just click
    and it's charging.
  </p>
  <p>
    I watched someone use one of these at a coffee shop in Barcelona before I got mine. They took a call, propped their
    phone up on the built-in kickstand, and the power bank just stayed attached the whole time. Looked effortless. Turns
    out it actually is.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The specs:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Capacity:</strong> 5,000mAh</li>
    <li><strong>Output:</strong> 7.5W wireless, 12W USB-C</li>
    <li><strong>Input:</strong> USB-C</li>
    <li><strong>Weight:</strong> 0.3 lbs (140g)</li>
    <li><strong>Dimensions:</strong> 4.1" × 2.6" × 0.5"</li>
    <li><strong>Works with:</strong> iPhone 12/13/14/15/16 (anything with MagSafe)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The magnetic thing is genuinely convenient. Just... click.</li>
    <li>Kickstand for video calls. Surprisingly useful</li>
    <li>Light enough that you barely notice it</li>
    <li>No cable hunting when you need a quick top-up</li>
    <li>Comes in colors that don't look terrible</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>5,000mAh gets you ONE charge. Maybe. Don't expect miracles.</li>
    <li>7.5W wireless is slow compared to plugging in</li>
    <li>iPhone only. Android folks, keep scrolling.</li>
    <li>Makes your phone noticeably thicker when attached</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Price:</strong> $50
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Who should get it:</strong> If you have an iPhone and hate fumbling with cables, this is the one. Perfect
    for meetings, all-day travel, or if you're the person who always hits 5% at the worst possible time.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/anker-622-magsafe"
      text="Check Anker 622 MagGo Price on Amazon"
      variant="primary"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <!-- Zendure SuperTank Pro -->
  <h3 id="zendure-supertank">3. Zendure SuperTank Pro . For the "I Need ALL the Power" Crowd</h3>
  <p>
    This is basically the most capacity you can bring on an airplane without getting questioned. 26,800mAh at
    96.48Wh. Juuust under the 100Wh TSA limit. Someone did the math.
  </p>
  <p>
    Built like a tank. Seriously. The aluminum body has survived being knocked off tables, shoved in overstuffed bags,
    and generally treated badly. No complaints so far.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The specs:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Capacity:</strong> 26,800mAh (96.48Wh)</li>
    <li><strong>Output:</strong> 100W USB-C × 2, 15W USB-A × 2</li>
    <li><strong>Input:</strong> 100W USB-C (full charge in 2 hours)</li>
    <li><strong>Ports:</strong> 2× USB-C, 2× USB-A</li>
    <li><strong>Weight:</strong> 1.4 lbs (650g)</li>
    <li><strong>Display:</strong> OLED that shows everything</li>
    <li><strong>TSA:</strong> Approved (barely under 100Wh)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Maximum legal capacity for flying. Smart design choice</li>
    <li>100W handles most laptops fine</li>
    <li>Four ports means you can charge basically everything at once</li>
    <li>OLED display is gorgeous and actually useful</li>
    <li>Pass-through charging (charge it while it charges your stuff)</li>
    <li>That aluminum build quality. Feels expensive because it is.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>$200. Yeah. That's a lot for a battery.</li>
    <li>1.4 lbs is heavy. You'll feel it in your bag</li>
    <li>100W won't charge a 16" MacBook Pro at full speed (need 140W for that)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Price:</strong> $200
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Who should get it:</strong> People who need maximum capacity and travel frequently. If you're regularly
