  <!-- Laptop Charging Requirements -->
  <h2 id="laptop-charging">Can This Thing Actually Charge My Laptop?</h2>
  <p>
    Short answer: maybe. It depends on watts, not just mAh. Here's what you actually need to know.
  </p>

  <h3>The Minimum for Laptop Charging</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>USB-C PD required:</strong> If the power bank doesn't say "PD" or "Power Delivery," it won't charge
      laptops. Period.
    </li>
    <li><strong>45W minimum:</strong> This charges most laptops when they're off or sleeping</li>
    <li><strong>65W is the sweet spot:</strong> Charges ultrabooks at full speed while using them</li>
    <li><strong>100W+ for big laptops:</strong> 16" MacBook Pro, gaming laptops, workstations</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What Does Your Laptop Actually Need?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>MacBook Air M3:</strong> 30W . basically any USB-C PD charger works</li>
    <li><strong>MacBook Pro 14":</strong> 70W . charges slower with less, but works</li>
    <li><strong>MacBook Pro 16":</strong> 140W . only the Anker 737 can do this at full speed</li>
    <li><strong>Dell XPS 13:</strong> 45-65W</li>
    <li><strong>ThinkPad X1 Carbon:</strong> 65W</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Check your laptop's charger brick. The wattage is printed on it somewhere.
  </p>

  <!-- TSA Regulations -->
  <h2 id="tsa-regulations">Don't Get Your Power Bank Confiscated at Security</h2>
  <p>
    I've seen people have their power banks taken at airport security. It's not fun. Here's how to avoid that.
  </p>

  <h3>The Rules That Actually Matter</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>CARRY-ON ONLY:</strong> Power banks are NEVER allowed in checked bags. They will confiscate them and you
      won't get them back.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Under 100Wh:</strong> No questions asked, just put it in your bag</li>
    <li><strong>100-160Wh:</strong> Technically needs airline approval, but I've never been asked</li>
    <li><strong>Over 160Wh:</strong> Don't even try. These get confiscated.</li>
    <li><strong>Quantity:</strong> Most airlines allow 2 per passenger</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Quick Math (So You Don't Have to)</h3>
  <p>
    Power banks list mAh on the box. Airlines care about Wh. Here's the conversion: <strong>Wh = mAh × 3.7 /
    1000</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>10,000mAh = 37Wh . fine everywhere</li>
    <li>20,000mAh = 74Wh . fine everywhere</li>
    <li>26,800mAh = 99.16Wh . sneaks under the limit (Zendure designed this on purpose)</li>
    <li>27,000mAh = 99.9Wh . cutting it close but legal</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    All eight power banks in this guide are under 100Wh. You're good.
  </p>

  <!-- Fast Charging -->
  <h2 id="fast-charging">Fast Charging Standards (The Confusing Part)</h2>
  <p>
    There are like five different "fast charging" standards and they all have annoying names. Here's the only stuff that
    actually matters:
  </p>

  <h3>USB-C PD (Power Delivery)</h3>
  <p>
    This is the one you want. Works with iPhones, Android, MacBooks, Windows laptops, tablets, everything. Ranges from
    18W to 240W depending on the charger. It's the universal standard and the only one I actually care about.
  </p>

  <h3>Quick Charge (Qualcomm)</h3>
  <p>
    Mostly for Android phones. QC 3.0 goes up to 18W, QC 4+ up to 27W. Most USB-C PD chargers work with Quick Charge
    devices anyway, so don't stress about this.
  </p>

  <h3>MagSafe</h3>
  <p>
    Apple's magnetic wireless charging thing. iPhones 12 and newer. 15W max with Apple-certified chargers, 7.5W with
    cheaper third-party options. Convenient but slower than plugging in.
  </p>

  <h3>What Should You Actually Look For?</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>USB-C PD.</strong> Just get something with USB-C PD. It works with basically everything and provides the
    fastest wired charging. Done.
  </p>

